d3d11: tessellation followed by a geometry shader

Applies on top of 0010.

The hull shader runs in the object stage and the domain shader in the mesh
stage, so a (non-pass-through) geometry shader had no stage left and such
draws were refused (UnsupportedGeometryTessellationDraw).  They now take
two passes inside the render pass:

1. The tessellation pipeline, compiled with SM50_SHADER_TESSELLATION_STREAM
   and rasterization off, streams each workload's domain shader output
   (every output register, reserved per workload with one atomic) and its
   primitives as indices into it (dxmt.domain_stream_primitives.*, the same
   triangle/line/point generators) to temporary buffers, counting them into
   indexed draw arguments and mesh dispatch arguments in the argument
   buffer (air_tessellation.metal documents the layout).
2. After a mesh -> object memory barrier, the geometry pipeline draws them
   with the indirect geometry draw; its object stage
   (convert_tessellation_stream_for_geometry_shader) is the vertex-for-GS
   stage with the vertex shader replaced by a copy of the streamed
   registers, so the geometry shader's mesh function is unchanged.

Both pipelines bind their stage tables at the mesh stage: the encoder keeps
the domain and geometry stages' last offsets, re-encodes both for every such
draw and switches between them.  Indirect tessellation draws with a geometry
shader are still skipped (their patch count is on the GPU).  The probe's
tess-then-geometry matches WARP (19 edge pixels of 16577), also with the
geometry shader reading its own constant buffer.

